& DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2":"2026-08-01","20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4":"2026-08-01","20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3":"2026-08-12","20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q5":"2026-10-26","20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q6":"2026-08-23","20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q1":"2026-10-26"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"The three pillars & how to measure","items":[{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q1","q":"How do metrics, logs, and traces differ, and when should each be used?","a":"There is no fixed count of “pillars.” Choose signals by the question, decision, and risk: metrics, logs, and traces are common, while profiles, events, continuous verification, and business-correctness signals may be the better fit. Name the signal's owner, cost, retention, and failure behavior.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Signal</th><th>Useful for</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Is a journey healthy?</td><td>metrics</td><td>rate, error ratio, latency, queue age</td><td>aggregate; cannot identify one request</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What happened?</td><td>logs and events</td><td>discrete context, audit, state transitions</td><td>volume, privacy, and search cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where did this request go?</td><td>traces</td><td>causal path and dependency timing</td><td>sampling, propagation gaps, and storage cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Why is CPU, lock, or allocation high?</td><td>profiles</td><td>hot code, lock, and allocation evidence</td><td>capture overhead and sensitive-data handling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Did the business outcome become correct and fresh?</td><td>business signals</td><td>correctness, freshness, durability, and reconciliation</td><td>domain-specific contract and denominator</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThe [[g:standard investigation flow]] is: a metric triggers an alert (p99 spikes) → a trace identifies the slow span → that span's logs explain why.\n\n:::deep\n- a [[r:common mistake]] is deriving metrics from logs, such as counting error lines to alert. It is expensive, slow, and breaks when logs are rate-limited. The code should emit counters and histograms directly.\n- a metric is a [[o:lossy aggregation]]: it cannot identify one specific request. That is the trace's job.\n- an [[g:exemplar]] in Prometheus and OTel attaches a trace ID to a histogram observation, allowing an operator to jump directly from a p99 spike on a dashboard to the trace that caused it. Mentioning this is a strong interview signal.\n- [[o:continuous profiling]] with tools such as Pyroscope or Parca answers 'which line of code consumes CPU?', something a trace cannot reveal (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q3)\n- [[o:events]] and business-correctness signals answer state-transition, freshness, reconciliation, or audit questions that aggregate telemetry cannot answer\n- signals should share bounded navigation dimensions such as `service`, `env`, and `version`; do not force user IDs, order IDs, or trace IDs into high-volume metric labels\n\n**Signal budget worksheet (illustrative fields, not provider prices):**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Input</th><th>Record before rollout</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Environment / region / provider</td><td><code>prod / ap-southeast-1 / OTel Collector + chosen backend</code></td><td>residency, ingestion pricing, limits, and ownership differ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume and shape</td><td>peak and p95 requests/events per second; spans per request</td><td>turn traffic into bytes, series, and collector queue estimates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cardinality</td><td>active series and bounded values per label</td><td>check backend limits and the effect of route, status, tenant, and version</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retention and sampling</td><td>metrics/logs/traces/profiles days; head/tail rates</td><td>price and investigation coverage are policy choices</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Failure budget</td><td>maximum telemetry loss, queue memory, export delay</td><td>keep telemetry from blocking or exhausting the application</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nReplace every example with measured values for the target region and provider. Cost and retention are not properties of the signal type alone.\n\nIn an OTA, flight-search p99 spikes → a trace shows that the GDS call consumes 90% of the request → the logs for that span reveal three retries after timeouts. Without a trace, the backend and integration teams can spend hours blaming each other.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n**What would change this answer** — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it:\n\n- The actual user journeys and correctness or freshness invariants that should become SLIs — beyond HTTP status and latency.\n- The OTel semantic-convention version, SDK and collector distribution, backend, region, retention and sampling policy in scope; every threshold, provider cost and collector setting on this page is illustrative until audited against a real deployment.\n- The cardinality, event volume, queue and memory, and telemetry-loss budgets acceptable per service and environment.\n- How the alerting path is monitored when the primary metrics or log backend is down, and which data is personal, financial or security-sensitive — and who may read raw telemetry.\n- What falsifies an SLO-based page: too little traffic, a manipulated denominator, a missing critical flow, or a known correctness failure the SLI cannot see.\n- What falsifies tail sampling: collector decision latency or memory beyond budget, errors dropped before the decision point, or backend cost forcing an unsafe rate.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2","q":"How does OpenTelemetry propagate trace context across services and through message queues?","a":"A trace is a causally related set of spans. Over synchronous HTTP/RPC, W3C Trace Context commonly carries the active context and the server span becomes a child of the client span.\n\n<pre><code>traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01\n             ^  ^                                ^                ^\n             |  trace-id                         parent span-id   flags\n             version</code></pre>\n\nPin the OpenTelemetry SDK, Collector distribution, propagator, and semantic-convention version used by the deployment.\n\nThe source review used semantic-conventions 1.44.0 as a dated snapshot; it is not a universal target, because conventions have mixed stability and migration guidance. Record the schema URL/version and an upgrade policy before relying on attribute names.\n\nAt the producer boundary, inject context once into message properties/headers with the configured propagator; at each consumer boundary, extract it once and model send, receive, process, and settle according to the pinned OpenTelemetry messaging conventions.\n\n<figure class='dgm'><svg viewBox='0 0 680 258' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' font-family='IBM Plex Mono, monospace' font-size='10.5' text-anchor='middle'>\n<rect x='0' y='0' width='680' height='258' rx='8' fill='#FBFCFE'/>\n<rect x='20' y='26' width='170' height='62' rx='8' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='105' y='51' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>checkout</text><text x='105' y='70' fill='#42536C'>client span</text>\n<rect x='490' y='26' width='170' height='62' rx='8' fill='#E7EAF6' stroke='#3D4E8F'/><text x='575' y='51' fill='#3D4E8F' font-weight='600'>payments</text><text x='575' y='70' fill='#42536C'>server span (child)</text>\n<path d='M192 57H484' stroke='#3D4E8F' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#tc202b)'/><text x='338' y='49' fill='#3D4E8F'>HTTP · traceparent: 00-4bf9…-00f0…-01</text><text x='338' y='75' fill='#42536C'>B's server span is a child of A's span</text>\n<rect x='255' y='146' width='170' height='62' rx='8' fill='#FFF4D9' stroke='#C58A19'/><text x='340' y='171' fill='#76500C' font-weight='600'>orders topic</text><text x='340' y='190' fill='#42536C'>headers: traceparent</text>\n<rect x='490' y='146' width='170' height='62' rx='8' fill='#F1EAF7' stroke='#7C5AA6'/><text x='575' y='167' fill='#5E3E80' font-weight='600'>fulfilment</text><text x='575' y='184' fill='#42536C'>process span</text><text x='575' y='199' fill='#42536C'>link → creation ctx</text>\n<path d='M150 90L251 168' stroke='#C58A19' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#tc202y)'/><text x='120' y='150' fill='#76500C'>inject into</text><text x='120' y='164' fill='#76500C'>record headers</text>\n<path d='M427 177H484' stroke='#7C5AA6' stroke-width='1.7' marker-end='url(#tc202p)'/><text x='457' y='170' fill='#5E3E80'>extract</text>\n<text x='340' y='242' fill='#14233A' font-weight='600'>One trace-id · sync hop = parent–child · queue hop = span link</text>\n<defs><marker id='tc202b' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#3D4E8F'/></marker><marker id='tc202y' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#C58A19'/></marker><marker id='tc202p' markerWidth='9' markerHeight='9' refX='7' refY='3' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 z' fill='#7C5AA6'/></marker></defs>\n</svg><figcaption>A synchronous hop makes the server span a child. A queue hop carries the same context in record headers, and the consumer links to it — the shape that stays honest for batches and fan-out.</figcaption></figure>\n\n**Which relation to record:**\n- one message: a parent–child span can be valid when the instrumentation contract allows it\n- a batch: link every message-creation context\n- fan-out: each consumer span links to the creation context, rather than inventing one consumer as the parent of its siblings\n- redelivery: retain a stable message identity and record the attempt, without pretending that every attempt is a new causal root\n\nExact span names and attributes remain SDK/instrumentation/version-specific.\n\n<pre><code>propagator.<span class=\"f\">inject</span>(Context.<span class=\"f\">current</span>(), record.<span class=\"f\">headers</span>(),\n    (headers, key, value) -&gt; headers.<span class=\"f\">add</span>(key, value.<span class=\"f\">getBytes</span>(UTF_8)));</code></pre>\n\n:::warn Links versus parent–child\nA [[g:span link]] records asynchronous causality without forcing a batch or fan-out into one misleading parent tree. Parent–child is a choice for a single-message process only when the pinned convention and instrumentation conditions allow it.\n:::\n\nFor a batch, link every message context; selecting one message as the parent misrepresents the rest. For fan-out, create independent consumer spans that link to the producer/message-creation context.\n\nTest redelivery and retries separately so correlation remains useful without double-counting business work.\n:::deep\nPropagation is a contract: standardize propagators, header size, trust boundaries, retries/redelivery, and what happens when headers are absent or malformed. Do not allow an untrusted external trace header to overwrite security identity; tracing context is correlation, not authentication.\n\nHead sampling decides early and is operationally cheap; tail sampling decides after collectors see enough spans and can retain policy-selected slow/error traces, but costs buffering and requires all relevant spans to reach the decision point.\n\nTail sampling cannot recover spans already dropped by an upstream head sampler. Choose rates from traffic, investigation value, privacy, and cost—“keep 100% of every slow/error trace” is not universally affordable or even well-defined.\n\nContext can be lost at executors, callbacks, reactive boundaries, custom clients, and message libraries; virtual threads do not remove that need. Test one trace across each real boundary.\n\nThe boundary test matrix should cover HTTP ingress/egress, one message, batch, fan-out, redelivery, missing/malformed headers, executor/callback/reactive hops, and exporter loss. Assert that an untrusted trace header cannot change the authenticated principal or authorization decision.\n\nPut `trace_id` in structured logs, keep baggage small and allowlisted, and never propagate PII/secrets merely for convenience (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3","q":"How do RED and USE guide metric selection for services and resources?","a":"RED and USE are measurement lenses, not a fixed SLI set or a law. Start with the user journey and the decision the signal must support; then apply each lens where it fits instead of measuring everything without knowing what to inspect.\n\n[[g:RED]] is for **services** that handle requests; [[g:USE]] is for **resources** such as CPU, disks, pools, and queues:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Signal</th><th>Measures</th><th>Java-service example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>RED</td><td><strong>R</strong>ate</td><td>requests per second</td><td><code>http_server_requests_seconds_count</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>RED</td><td><strong>E</strong>rrors</td><td>failed requests per second and error ratio</td><td>5xx ratio per route template</td></tr>\n<tr><td>RED</td><td><strong>D</strong>uration</td><td>the distribution of processing time, using a histogram rather than an average</td><td>p99 from <code>_bucket</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>USE</td><td><strong>U</strong>tilization</td><td>percentage of time busy</td><td><code>hikaricp_connections_active</code> over <code>_max</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>USE</td><td><strong>S</strong>aturation</td><td>queued demand, such as run queue, pool wait, or lag</td><td><code>hikaricp_connections_pending</code>, consumer lag</td></tr>\n<tr><td>USE</td><td><strong>E</strong>rrors</td><td>failures in the resource itself</td><td><code>hikaricp_connections_timeout_total</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nA minimum service dashboard has RED for critical endpoints plus USE for the connection pool, thread pool, and consumer lag.\n\nThe SLO may measure availability or latency, but it may instead need freshness, correctness, queue age, durability, or a business outcome; RED/USE should explain that contract, not replace it.\n\n:::tip A useful closing line\n*'High utilization is not necessarily painful; [[o:saturation]] is what the customer experiences.'* A CPU at 90% with an empty run queue is healthy—it is using the capacity you paid for.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[r:do not measure averages]]. An average of 200 ms may combine 95% of requests at 50 ms with 5% at 3 s. Use histograms and inspect p50/p95/p99.\n- [[r:do not average percentiles]] across instances. Adding the p99 of ten pods and dividing by ten produces a meaningless number. Aggregate histogram buckets first, then calculate the percentile (`histogram_quantile` over `rate(..._bucket[5m])` performs that aggregation).\n- measure latency [[o:separately for successes and errors]]: failed requests often fail fast and pull p99 down, hiding the real problem\n- coordinated omission occurs when server-side measurement misses time spent waiting outside its queue; add client-side or load-balancer measurements\n- Count a logical user request and a retry attempt according to the SLI contract; otherwise retries can inflate rate and error numbers or hide an unknown outcome.\n- useful Java-service saturation signals include `hikaricp.connections.pending`, thread-pool queue length, GC pause time, and Kafka consumer lag. These four catch most incidents.\n- Google's [[g:four golden signals]] are RED plus saturation. Being able to connect both frameworks is sufficient.\n\n**Concrete PromQL for one HTTP service (adapt metric names to the instrumentation):**\n<pre><code># RED — request rate by bounded route template\nsum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m]))\n# RED — 5xx ratio\nsum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=~\"5..\"}[5m]))\n/\nclamp_min(sum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])), 1e-9)\n# RED — p99 from a classic histogram; keep le during aggregation\nhistogram_quantile(0.99,\n  sum by (route, le) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m])))\n# USE — utilization of the JDBC pool (busy share, not yet pain)\nmax by (pool) (hikaricp_connections_active / hikaricp_connections_max)\n# USE — saturation: threads waiting for a connection\nmax by (pool) (hikaricp_connections_pending)</code></pre>\n\n**Impact:**\n\n- **Benefit:** the queries make “watch latency” testable and alertable.\n- **Cost:** p99 is estimated from bucket boundaries; raw URL, user or trace-id labels create unbounded series.\n- **Boundary:** metrics reveal symptoms and pressure; traces/logs explain the request and dependency that caused them.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q4","q":"Why can one bad label cause a cardinality explosion that takes down the entire metrics system?","a":"[[b:Cardinality]] is the number of distinct time series. In a Prometheus-style backend, each label combination creates a series and active-series memory is a real limit; exact storage, ingestion, and billing behavior is provider-specific.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># 1 series — safe</span>\nhttp_requests_total{service=\"booking\"}\n<span class=\"c\">#</span>\n<span class=\"c\"># 5 endpoints x 4 statuses x 3 methods = 60 series — still safe</span>\nhttp_requests_total{service=\"booking\", endpoint=\"/bookings\", status=\"200\", method=\"POST\"}\n<span class=\"c\">#</span>\n<span class=\"c\"># + user_id (1 million users) = 60,000,000 series — failure</span>\nhttp_requests_total{service=\"booking\", ..., user_id=\"u_8412773\"}</code></pre>\n\n[[r:Labels with unbounded values]] are almost always the cause: `user_id`, `booking_id`, `email`, `trace_id`, full URLs containing path IDs, or exception messages.\n\n**Prevention:**\n- label values must come from a [[g:finite, known set]]\n- normalise paths to `/bookings/{id}`, not `/bookings/8H2K`; [[r:this is the most common mistake]]\n- high-dimensional analysis by user or booking belongs in [[g:logs or traces]], not metrics\n- configure provider-specific scrape/ingestion/series limits; `sample_limit` bounds one scrape but does not replace backend cardinality budgets or query protection\n\n**Cardinality, cost, and retention worksheet (illustrative fields):**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Record by environment/region/provider</th><th>Decision or test</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Metric series</td><td>active series, label value counts, histogram buckets, churn from deploys</td><td>product of bounded dimensions stays under the backend budget</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Logs</td><td>events/sec, bytes/event, sampled rate, indexed fields, retention days</td><td>estimate ingest/index cost and keep high-cardinality detail out of labels</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Traces/profiles</td><td>spans/request, fan-out, head/tail rate, profile frequency, retention</td><td>bound collector memory and decide what evidence must survive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Region/provider</td><td>residency, pricing unit, quota, deletion/legal-hold rule</td><td>do not copy a cost or retention number between providers</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n:::deep\n- a quick estimate: a metric's cardinality is the [[o:product]] of the value counts for all labels. Adding a label with ten values multiplies cardinality by ten; it does not add ten.\n- symptoms include steadily growing Prometheus RAM until OOM, progressively slower queries, and increasing `scrape_duration`. Diagnose with `topk(10, count by (__name__)({__name__=~\".+\"}))` and the `/api/v1/status/tsdb` endpoint.\n- using an exception message as a label is a subtle trap: `error=\"connection timeout to 10.0.3.41:5432\"` makes each IP and port combination a new series. Use a classified [[g:error type]] such as `error=\"timeout\"`.\n- histogram `le` buckets also multiply cardinality: a 12-bucket histogram creates 12 series plus two. Do not add a histogram to an already highly labeled metric.\n- continuous deployments plus a `version` label create churn because old series consume RAM until retention expires. Consider removing version from high-frequency metrics.\n- in systems billed by custom metric, such as Datadog or CloudWatch, a cardinality explosion may not crash the platform; it [[r:explodes the bill]], sometimes discovered a month later\n\n**Pre-rollout tests:**\n- reject user/order/trace IDs, raw URLs, exception messages, and secrets as metric labels\n- generate representative traffic and assert series growth stays under the budget\n- assert route normalization\n- emit synthetic PII/token/payment fields and verify source redaction\n- verify retention/deletion and legal-hold behavior\n\nKeep trace IDs in controlled detail or exemplars, not primary labels, and test that forged trace context cannot authorize a request.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"}]},{"title":"SLOs, alerting & incidents","items":[{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1","q":"What decisions should SLI, SLO, SLA, and an error budget drive?","a":"- [[b:SLI]] is a measured user-relevant outcome. It may be availability/latency, freshness, correctness, queue age, durability, or a business success measure; for example, the ratio of valid booking requests completed successfully within 300 ms.\n- [[b:SLO]] is the target and measurement window for an SLI, plus its inclusion/exclusion rules.\n- [[b:SLA]] is an external agreement; consequences may include service credits, termination rights, reporting, or other remedies—not necessarily a financial penalty in every contract.\n- An [[b:error budget]] is the allowed bad portion. The 99.9%/43m12s arithmetic is an example for a time-based objective over exactly 30 days; a request-based objective allows 0.1% of eligible requests, not 43 minutes.\n\nUse budget and burn-rate data to govern risk: release pace, experiment exposure, maintenance, reliability work, and escalation.\n\nThe response to exhausted budget is a policy agreed with product and engineering; it can include slowing risky changes, narrowing rollout, or prioritizing reliability, but an automatic company-wide “feature freeze” is not universal.\n\n**What the objective buys in this illustrative 30-day calculation (43,200 minutes; not a universal target):**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Objective</th><th>Time-based budget / 30d</th><th>Request-based, at 1M req/day</th><th>What that buys</th></tr>\n<tr><td><code>99%</code></td><td>7h 12m</td><td>300,000 bad requests</td><td>a comfortable internal tool</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>99.5%</code></td><td>3h 36m</td><td>150,000</td><td>a normal internal service</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>99.9%</code></td><td><b>43m 12s</b></td><td>30,000</td><td>one possible public-facing policy; validate with journey data</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>99.95%</code></td><td>21m 36s</td><td>15,000</td><td>needs redundancy, not just care</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>99.99%</code></td><td><b>4m 19s</b></td><td>3,000</td><td>shorter than most human responses; it has to be automatic</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n**Request-based counterexample:** a 10-minute outage at 1,000 eligible requests/second creates about 600,000 bad requests.\n- a time-based 30-day SLO may still pass, because 10 minutes is below 43m12s\n- a 99.9% request SLO with 1M requests/day has only 30,000 bad requests of budget for the month\n\nThis is why the journey, denominator, and window must be stated together.\n\nRead the last row before promising it. At `99.99%` a single failed deploy that takes five minutes to roll back has spent the entire month, so the objective is really a statement about automation, not about effort.\n\n**Burn rate is the number that makes an SLO alertable.** It is how fast the budget is being spent relative to spending it exactly evenly. The windows and thresholds below are examples derived from the stated 30-day objective, not universal alert settings:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">burn rate = observed error ratio / (1 - SLO)</span>\n<span class=\"c\"></span>\n<span class=\"c\">for a 99.9% objective the budget is a 0.1% error ratio, so:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">  observed 0.1%  -> burn 1x    the budget lasts exactly 30 days</span>\n<span class=\"c\">  observed 1.44% -> burn 14.4x the budget is gone in ~2 days</span>\n<span class=\"c\">  observed 10%   -> burn 100x  the budget is gone in ~7 hours</span></code></pre>\n\n**Alert on burn rate with two windows, never on a raw threshold.** The long window decides that something is really wrong; the short window makes the alert stop when the incident does:\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Long window</th><th>Short window</th><th>Burn rate</th><th>Budget spent before it fires</th><th>Action</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 hour</td><td>5 min</td><td>14.4×</td><td>2%</td><td><b>page</b> — fast burn, someone is losing money now</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6 hours</td><td>30 min</td><td>6×</td><td>5%</td><td><b>page</b> — sustained, will exhaust the month</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 day</td><td>2 hours</td><td>3×</td><td>10%</td><td>ticket — real, not urgent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 days</td><td>6 hours</td><td>1×</td><td>10%</td><td>ticket — the slow leak nobody notices</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># both windows must be burning, or a five-minute blip pages someone at 3am</span>\n- alert: HighErrorBudgetBurn\n  expr: |\n    (\n      job:slo_errors:ratio_rate1h{job=\"checkout\"}  &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)\n      <span class=\"k\">and</span>\n      job:slo_errors:ratio_rate5m{job=\"checkout\"}  &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)\n    )\n  <span class=\"k\">for</span>: 2m\n  labels: { severity: page }\n  annotations:\n    summary: <span class=\"s\">\"checkout is burning its error budget 14.4x — 2% spent in an hour\"</span></code></pre>\n\n:::tip Why two windows and not one\nA single long window is slow to fire and, worse, slow to *clear* — it keeps paging for hours after the incident is fixed, which is how a team learns to ignore the alert. A single short window fires on every blip. The short window is the reset switch.\n:::\n\n:::warn Separate availability from correctness\nA 100% availability target is usually unrealistic and produces no learning budget.\n:::\n\nSome correctness invariants—no unbalanced ledger entry, no unauthorized transfer—are intentionally absolute and need transactional controls, reconciliation, and audit rather than being diluted into an availability SLO.\n:::deep\nMeasure at multiple meaningful points: client/edge for user experience and service boundaries for diagnosis. Define eligible events, “good,” planned maintenance, retries, partial responses, low traffic, and data latency so teams cannot reinterpret the SLO during an incident.\n\nRequest-based SLIs suit event volume; time-window SLIs can be useful for sparse traffic but hide burst size. Segment journeys with materially different expectations instead of averaging critical checkout with history browsing.\n\nDependency SLO multiplication is only a rough model under serial, independent, no-mitigation assumptions. Redundancy, caching, hedging, fallbacks, correlated failures, and traffic split change end-to-end availability.\n\nMeasure the composed user journey and use dependency models for design, not as a mathematical ceiling.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q2","q":"How should alerts be designed to avoid fatigue: should they fire on symptoms or causes?","a":"Page on a condition that is urgent, actionable, and important. User-visible symptoms and fast error-budget burn are strong defaults because they aggregate many causes.\n\nCause-based signals can still justify a page when they predict imminent impact and require immediate action—for example exhausted queue capacity, a certificate about to expire, data-loss risk, or a failed redundancy layer before the last copy fails.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Contract</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Page</td><td>a named responder must act now to limit material impact</td><td>fast multi-window SLO burn, ledger invariant breach</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ticket/work queue</td><td>action has an owner and due window but can wait for business hours</td><td>slow budget burn, capacity forecast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dashboard/event</td><td>context for diagnosis; no guaranteed action</td><td>CPU, pod restart, GC cycle</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nMulti-window, multi-burn-rate alerts pair a short window for fast incidents with a longer confirmation window for sustained burn.\n\nValues such as 14.4× or 6× are examples derived from an SLO window and desired exhaustion time, not universal thresholds; calculate them for the actual objective and traffic.\n\n**Operational multi-window table (example policy to tune):**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Condition</th><th>Example windows</th><th>Traffic floor</th><th>Owner / runbook / action</th><th>If monitoring is down</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fast user-impact burn</td><td>1h + 5m at 14.4×</td><td>Enough eligible requests in both windows; example floor: 100 in the short window</td><td>service on-call; rollback/degrade runbook; mitigate now</td><td>independent heartbeat pages monitoring owner; use manual escalation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sustained burn</td><td>6h + 30m at 6×</td><td>confirm denominator is non-zero and representative</td><td>service owner; capacity/release runbook; act before budget exhaustion</td><td>black-box journey and last-known SLI; do not interpret stale data as zero</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slow burn</td><td>1d + 2h at 3×</td><td>route to ticket when volume supports a meaningful estimate</td><td>service owner; capacity/reliability ticket; fix in business window</td><td>record missing-data ticket and inspect independent trend/heartbeat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing evaluator or notification</td><td>heartbeat/evaluation age beyond contract</td><td>not a request-volume alert; synthetic check is the floor</td><td>monitoring owner; monitoring-path runbook; restore alert delivery</td><td>page through an independent channel and retain manual escalation</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nThe traffic floor is an anti-noise guard, not permission to ignore low-volume correctness, security, or freshness signals; use a synthetic journey or an absolute invariant for those cases.\n\n:::deep\nEvery routed alert needs an owner, severity, tested notification path, evidence, and a first safe action. A runbook is especially valuable for operational pages, but a security or correctness alert may instead require a restricted incident procedure.\n\nTune `for` duration to the failure mode. Five minutes may suppress noise but can be unacceptable for rapid data loss; use multi-window logic, hysteresis, grouping, inhibition, and deduplication based on evidence.\n\n“Two non-actionable alerts” is a review trigger, not an automatic delete if the signal reveals a missing capability.\n\nMeasure pages, acknowledgment, actionable rate, duplicate volume, escalation, and missed incidents per rotation. There is no universal sustainable pages-per-week number: staffing, duration, severity, follow-up load, and recovery opportunity all matter.\n\nAlso detect abnormal silence with expected-traffic or heartbeat signals, especially for low-error systems.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q3","q":"What is the incident-response process from the initial page through the postmortem?","a":"Incident response optimizes safe impact reduction while preserving the evidence and control needed for the incident type.\n\n1. **Declare and scope:** record impact, affected journeys/tenants/data, severity, incident lead, and communication cadence.\n2. **Stabilize:** choose the lowest-risk mitigation supported by evidence—rollback, disable a flag, shed/degrade load, fail over, revoke credentials, or isolate a component. A recent deployment is a strong clue, not proof that immediate rollback is always safe.\n3. **Coordinate:** separate incident command, technical operations, communications, and subject expertise when incident size permits. Small teams may combine roles, but one person owns decisions and the timeline.\n4. **Verify recovery:** check user SLIs, queues/backlogs, data integrity, security containment, and downstream health; “graphs turned green” may not mean work or data is correct.\n5. **Transition:** preserve artifacts, define monitoring/reconciliation, communicate status, and hand off follow-up risk.\n6. **Closure gate:** close only when every condition below holds. If any remains unknown, keep the incident in monitoring, recovery, or reconciliation status with an owner and next check.\n\n**Closure conditions:**\n- the user SLI has recovered and held for the agreed verification window\n   - data correctness and reconciliation are checked\n- security/privacy containment is verified\n- queues/backlogs and customer-visible state are accounted for\n- telemetry/evaluator/notification paths are healthy\n\n:::warn Availability and security can differ\nFor a routine availability incident, restore service before deep root-cause analysis. For suspected compromise, fraud, or data corruption, blindly restoring or destroying evidence can increase harm.\n:::\n\nFollow the security/data incident procedure, contain access, and involve legal/privacy/compliance roles where required.\n:::deep\nThe learning review should be blameless and evidence-based: timeline, contributing technical/organizational conditions, detection and decision quality, impact, what helped, what made recovery harder, and actions with owners and verification dates.\n\n“Five whys” is one prompt, not a mandatory proof of one root cause; complex incidents usually have interacting causes and failed defenses.\n\nTrack metrics only when they drive improvement. Time to detect/acknowledge/mitigate/recover, customer harm, data reconciliation, and recurrence can be useful, but a single MTTR average hides severity and encourages premature closure. Review distributions and qualitative decision points.\n\nCorrective actions should reduce recurrence probability, blast radius, detection time, or recovery risk. Exercise rollback/failover, credential rotation, backups/restores, degraded modes, and communication through game days.\n\nFintech/OTA recovery always includes reconciliation for stuck bookings, duplicate attempts, and ambiguous payment outcomes.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4","q":"How should structured logging and correlation be designed so logs are actually searchable?","a":"Structured logging starts with a versioned event schema, not with “turn every string into JSON.” A useful event normally includes a timestamp, severity, service/environment/version, a stable event name, outcome, and the fields needed to investigate that event.\n\nAdd `trace_id` and `span_id` when a valid span is active; background jobs and bootstrap logs may legitimately have neither.\n\n<pre><code>{\"ts\":\"2026-07-31T10:04:12Z\",\"severity\":\"WARN\",\"service\":\"booking\",\n \"version\":\"2026.07.31.2\",\"event\":\"seat_hold_failed\",\"outcome\":\"rejected\",\n \"trace_id\":\"4bf92f3577b34da6\",\"seat\":\"12A\",\"flight\":\"VN231\",\n \"reason\":\"already_held\",\"duration_ms\":42}</code></pre>\n\nUse stable field names and bounded classifications such as `reason=\"already_held\"`; keep free-form detail in a message field. Correlation IDs, tenant IDs, and business IDs should be allowlisted according to privacy and cardinality policy rather than copied into every event.\n\n:::tip Context lifecycle\nInstall trace context at the ingress or job boundary and remove or restore only the keys that boundary owns.\n:::\n\nA scoped MDC API can make cleanup explicit: <pre><code><span class=\"k\">try</span> (MDC.MDCCloseable ignored = MDC.<span class=\"f\">putCloseable</span>(<span class=\"s\">\"trace_id\"</span>, traceId)) { chain.<span class=\"f\">doFilter</span>(req, res); }</code></pre> Test propagation across the actual executor, virtual-thread, callback, reactive, and messaging boundaries. A `ThreadLocal`-backed MDC does not by itself establish cross-boundary propagation.\n:::deep\n- Define severities as an operational contract. `ERROR` should represent a failed operation or condition that is actionable under the team's policy; it does not universally imply that a human must inspect every occurrence.\n- Sample or rate-limit noisy events using a risk- and cost-based policy. Security/audit events may require separate immutable retention; “100% failures and 1% successes” is not a universal safe ratio. Metrics should record aggregate rates even when individual logs are sampled.\n- Redact or avoid credentials, session/token material, payment data, and unnecessary personal data at the source. Appender-side masking is defense in depth, not permission to emit secrets first.\n- Log an exception where ownership and outcome are known. Multiple layers may emit different useful events, but duplicating the same stack trace at every rethrow adds cost without evidence.\n- Containerized applications commonly write to `stdout` for platform collection, while direct OTLP export, sidecars, or managed file agents can also be valid. Choose one delivery contract and specify buffering, backpressure, loss behavior, and retention.\n\n**Telemetry contract tests:**\n- emit synthetic token, payment, address, and personal fields and assert redaction at the source\n- reject unexpected high-cardinality fields from metric labels\n- verify route normalization, required correlation fields, sampling behavior, and bounded buffering\n- exercise deletion and legal-hold retention paths\n\nA forged traceparent, tracestate, or baggage value must remain correlation data and must not change authentication or authorization decisions.\n\nTrace/log correlation and sampling mechanics are covered in (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2). Never treat trace context as authentication identity.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q5","difficulty":"advanced","q":"An API is healthy, but the queue, outbox, or reconciliation backlog is growing. What should the SLI and alert measure?","a":"Separate **request acceptance** from **work completion**. A 200 response can truthfully mean “the command was durably accepted” while the business outcome remains pending; it must not make a growing backlog invisible.\n\nVersion the SLI contract with the journey, owner, eligible population, good/bad/excluded events, clock/source, region, retry treatment, and alert query. The alert and SLO must use the same denominator; distinguish logical work from delivery attempts and keep unknown outcomes visible.\n\nFor asynchronous work, define the population and clock explicitly:\n- accepted commands or events as the denominator\n- age of the oldest item and p95/p99 completion age, not only average queue depth\n- accepted-to-completed success, retry/DLQ rate, and items stuck in an unknown state\n- outbox/CDC watermark, consumer lag, reconciliation delta, and the value/age of unresolved breaks\n- user-visible freshness or promise, such as “search reflects a catalog change within the stated window”\n\nPage when the user/business SLO is burning or a bounded capacity threshold predicts imminent loss, and route a ticket when the condition is actionable but not urgent. A queue with 10,000 old items can be worse than one with 100,000 young items; the age and invariant decide.\n\nDuring recovery, drain with an explicit rate budget, preserve idempotency and ordering scope, and verify counts, duplicates, missing work, domain invariants, and customer-visible status. “Queue depth returned to zero” is not proof if the consumer discarded or misapplied work.\n\n:::deep\nKeep the API SLI, worker SLI, freshness SLI, and reconciliation SLI separate, then publish a journey-level view that explains how they combine. Do not average them into one flattering number.\n:::"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q6","difficulty":"advanced","q":"How should observability fail without becoming a second outage, and how do you verify the monitoring path?","a":"Treat telemetry as a bounded dependency with its own health signals. An exporter outage, collector queue, cardinality spike, sampling rule, or backend limit must not exhaust application memory or block the critical request path.\n\n**Design the failure boundary:**\n- bound collector memory, batch size, queue depth, retry duration, and disk/WAL usage where persistence is justified\n- monitor accepted, dropped, failed, queued, and exported telemetry, plus collector CPU/memory and backend rejection\n- keep low-cardinality SLO metrics independent from high-cardinality traces and logs; prioritize error/latency evidence under pressure\n- redact credentials, tokens, payment data, addresses, and user content before export; sampling is not a privacy control\n- define a degraded mode: drop debug detail first, retain aggregate/business failure signals, and recover without replaying an unbounded storm\n\n**Crash-window checks:**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Window</th><th>Failure</th><th>Independent evidence or recovery</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Before instrumentation initializes</td><td>startup crash has no application telemetry</td><td>platform logs/exit code, startup diagnostic, and black-box SLI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Context or sampling boundary</td><td>trace splits or an error is dropped before tail sampling</td><td>propagation tests; independent error metrics/logs; tail sampling only when capacity allows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collector queue/cardinality pressure</td><td>telemetry drops, backend rejects, or memory grows</td><td>bounded queue/WAL, accepted-dropped-failed-exported counters, collector CPU/memory, and a pressure drill</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Evaluator/notification outage</td><td>no page while service or monitoring is failing</td><td>independent heartbeat, evaluator-age/missing-data alert, alternate notification channel, manual escalation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retention/deletion boundary</td><td>sensitive data remains or required evidence disappears</td><td>classification, access audit, deletion test, and separate legal-hold evidence path</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nVerify the monitoring path with a synthetic heartbeat, a deliberately generated known error, a collector/backend outage drill, and an alert-delivery test.\n\nRecord detection delay, dropped evidence, memory ceiling, recovery time, evaluator freshness, notification success, and which user-facing SLO remained observable.\n\nSelf-observe collectors, evaluators, and notification paths through signals that do not rely solely on the component or backend being tested.\n\nA green application dashboard during a telemetry outage is not proof of health; it may only prove that the dashboard has stopped receiving data. Alert on missing data and stale evaluation separately from zero-valued service metrics.\n\n:::warn The monitoring system is not the source of truth for its own availability\nUse an independent or lower-dependency check for alert delivery and retain a manual escalation path. The system that tells you monitoring is down must not depend on the same failing collector/backend.\n:::"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"20-observability-sre","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-b